===Etymology 1===
{{ety|la|:inh|itc-ola:labōs|id=work}}
From {{inh|la|itc-ola|labōs}}, of {{unc|la|nocap=1}} origin:

* Perhaps from a {{der|la|ine-pro|-}} root {{m|ine-pro|*leb-||to seize, take}}, whence {{cog|sa|लभते||take, gain}} and {{cog|lt|lõbis||wealth}}.{{rfv-etym|la}}
* More commonly connected with {{m|la|labō||to totter}}<ref>{{R:es:Roberts:2014}}</ref> (see Etymology 2 below), via semantic development "teetering under a heavy load" → "burden" → "work", but this is rejected by De Vaan, who however provides no alternative.<ref>{{R:itc:EDL|page=320|head=labor}}</ref>

The semantic connection is weak in either case.

* Prósper suggests a possible root {{m|ine-pro||*dh̥₂bʰ-ōs}}, cognate with {{cog|lt|dobti||to strike, beat, kill, {{q|dial.}} torment, exhaust}}. See source for more.<ref>{{cite-journal|1=en|year=2019|first=Blanca María|last=Prósper|title=What became of "Sabine l"? An overlooked Proto-Italic Sound Law|journal=The journal of Indo-European Studies|volume=47|issue=3/4|page=483|doi=}}</ref>

