* {{alt|gmw-cfr|jeen||westernmost [[Ripuarian]], other Ripuarian obsolete}}

===Etymology===
From {{inh|gmw-cfr|gmh||(en) kein}}, from {{m|gmh|nechein}}, from {{inh|gmw-cfr|goh|nehhein}}. Cognate with {{cog|de|kein}}, {{cog|nl|geen}}.

{{col-top|2|Phonetic development}}
When the form {{m|gmh||nechein}} was shortened, {{IPAchar|/x/}} came to stand in word-initial position, which violated the phonetic system of the time. In most of Continental West Germanic the rule applies that {{IPAchar|/x/}} was replaced with {{IPAchar|/ɣ/}} in dialects that allowed that sound word-initially, but with {{IPAchar|/k/}} in dialects that did not. Ripuarian belonged to the former group, Moselle Franconian to the latter. Accordingly, the native form throughout Ripuarian is {{m|gmw-cfr||jeen (jein)}} with regular {{IPAchar|/j < ʝ < ɣ/}}. The {{IPAchar|/k/}}-form spread northwards under Standard German influence, surely reinforced by the fact that the phonetic difference between {{m|gmw-cfr||een|t=one}} and {{m|gmw-cfr||jeen|t=no}} was undesirably slim. It appears that {{m|gmw-cfr||keen}} was first introduced by the generation(s) born in the early 19th century and that it became predominant by the end of that century. The only Ripuarian area that remained true to the older form was [[Aachen]] and surroundings.
{{col-bottom}}

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|gmw-cfr|/keːn/}}

