===Etymology 3===
Probably from {{derived|en|la|jugum|t=yoke, tether}}. A [[folk etymology]] claims that it is an acronym for "{{l|en|justice}} under {{l|en|God}}" or "{{l|en|judgment}} under God". [https://www.ignatius.edu/news/september-2017/jug-em-with-a-jugum][https://catholicphilly.com/2021/10/news/local-news/local-priest-moves-closer-to-sainthood/]

====Alternative forms====
* {{l|en|JUG}}

====Noun====
{{en-noun}}

# {{lb|en|US|Jesuit schools|countable|or|uncountable}} [[detention|Detention]] {{gloss|after-school student punishment}}.
#* {{quote-book|en|title=The Narrows|author=Kenneth H. Brown|publisher=The Deal Press|location=New York City| year=1970| url=https://books.google.com/books?hl=vi&id=XOnF76T1VfcC| platform=Google Books| passage=“Take a week’s '''Jug''',” he said, “and keep your nose clean.”}}
#* {{quote-book|en|title=Come To Believe: How the Jesuits are Reinventing Education (Again)| author=Stephen, N., S.J. Katsouros| publisher=w:Orbis Books| location=Maryknoll, New York| date=2017-06-12| page=27| isbn=978-1-60833-685-2| url=https://books.google.com/books?id=jZYnDwAAQBAJ&pg=PT27| platform=Google Books| passage=I had another role that earned me almost no appreciation at all: I served as the master after classes in the '''JUG''' room, where students appeared when they received detention.}}
#* {{quote-web|en|title=Jug 'Em with a Jugum| author=Tom Healey| work=Lessons from Loyola Hall| publisher=[[w:Saint Ignatius High School (Cleveland)|Saint Ignatius High School]]| location=Cleveland| date=September 2017| accessdate=2021-11-24| url=https://www.ignatius.edu/news/september-2017/jug-em-with-a-jugum|passage=In days gone by '''jugs''' included the memorization of [[Shakespeare]] or the writing out of some well-known document like the [[Constitution]].|archiveurl=https://web.archive.org/web/20250814101416/https://www.ignatius.edu/news/september-2017/jug-em-with-a-jugum|archivedate=14 August 2025}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|title=Slowik: Reunion reveals changes to high school, people and places in 35 years| author=Ted Slowik| work=w:Chicago Tribune| date=2018-10-16| accessdate=2021-11-24| url=https://www.chicagotribune.com/suburbs/daily-southtown/opinion/ct-sta-slowik-high-school-reunion-st-1017-story.html| passage=A common infraction that landed pupils in '''jug''' was getting caught using a stairwell that was reserved for use by faculty and other adults.|archiveurl=https://web.archive.org/web/20230409172721/https://www.chicagotribune.com/suburbs/daily-southtown/opinion/ct-sta-slowik-high-school-reunion-st-1017-story.html|archivedate=9 April 2023}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|title=A Look Back at JUGs|author=Steele Clevenger|work=The Jesuit Chronicle| publisher=[[w:Jesuit High School (Beaverton, Oregon)|Jesuit High School]]| location=Beaverton, Oregon| date=2020-03-08| accessdate=2021-11-24|url=https://jesuitnews.com/6346/features/a-look-back-at-jugs/| passage=In addition to '''JUGs''' and disciplinary lectures, spats and hacks, paddles used to smack misbehaving students, often went with receiving a '''JUG'''.|archiveurl=https://web.archive.org/web/20250709000422/https://jesuitnews.com/6346/features/a-look-back-at-jugs/|archivedate=9 July 2025}}

=====Usage notes=====
This is the preferred term for after-school detentions in Roman Catholic schools run by the [[Society of Jesus]] in the United States.

