===Noun===
{{en-noun|~}}

# {{lb|en|countable|[[Jewish#Adjective|Jewish]]|_|history}} A [[special#Adjective|special]] [[year]] of [[emancipation]] [[suppose]]d to be [[observe]]d every [[fifty]] years, when [[farming#Noun|farming]] was [[temporarily]] [[stop#Verb|stopped]], certain [[house#Noun|houses]] and [[land#Noun|land]] which had been [[sell#Verb|sold]] could be [[redeem]]ed by the [[original#Adjective|original]] [[owner]]s or their [[relative#Noun|relatives]], and [[Hebrew#Adjective|Hebrew]] [[slave#Noun|slaves]] [[set free]]. {{defdate|from late 14th c.}}
#* {{RQ:King James Version|Leviticus|25|8–10|page=213|column=2|passage=And thou ſhalt number ſeuen Sabbaths of yeeres vnto thee, ſeuen times ſeuen yeeres, and the ſpace of the ſeuen Sabbaths of yeeres, ſhall be vnto thee fourtie and nine yeeres. Then ſhalt thou cauſe the trumpet of the '''Jubile''' to ſound, on the tenth day of the ſeuenth moneth, in the day of atonement ſhall ye make the trumpet ſound throughout all your land. And ye ſhall hallow the fiftieth yeere, and proclaime libertie throughout all the land, vnto al the inhabitants thereof: It ſhalbe a '''Jubile''' vnto you, and ye ſhall returne euery man vnto his poſſeſſion, and ye ſhall returne euery man vnto his family.}}
#* {{RQ:Purchas Pilgrimes|part=5|chapter=Of the Festiuall Dayes Instituted by God in the Law|page=113|passage=Touching this yeere of '''''Iubilee''''' is much controverſie. The ancient Authors account it the fiftieth yeere. {{...}} ''Culuiſius'' hath at large diſputed this queſtion againſt ''Creutzhemius'' and ''Bucholcerus'', by diuers arguments prouing that the '''''Iubilee''''' vvas but fortie nine yeeres complete, and that the fiftieth yeere vvas the firſt onvvards of another '''''Iubilee''''' or Sabbath of yeeres: {{...}}}}
#* {{quote-book|en|author=w:Diarmaid MacCulloch|chapter=Boundaries Defined (50 CE–300)|title=[[w:A History of Christianity: The First Three Thousand Years|Christianity: The First Three Thousand Years]]|location=New York, N.Y.|publisher=[[w:Viking Books|Viking]]|year=2009|year_published=2010|page=120|pageurl=https://archive.org/details/christianityfirs0000macc/page/120/mode/1up|isbn=978-0-670-02126-0|passage=[I]n the old Israel, there had supposedly been a system of ‘'''Jubilee'''’, a year in which all land should go back to the family to which it had originally belonged and during which all slaves should be released.}}
# {{lb|en|countable|by extension}}
## {{lb|en|Roman Catholicism}} A special year (originally held every [[hundred#Numeral|hundred]] years, then at more [[frequent#Adjective|frequent]] [[interval]]s, and now [[declarable]] by the [[Pope]] at any [[time#Noun|time]] and also for [[period#Noun|periods]] less than a year) in which [[plenary#Adjective|plenary]] [[indulgence]]s and [[remission]] from [[sin#Noun|sin]] can be [[grant#Verb|granted]] upon [[make#Verb|making]] a [[pilgrimage#Noun|pilgrimage]] to [[Rome]] or other [[designated#Adjective|designated]] [[church#Noun|churches]]. {{defdate|from 15th c.}}
## A [[major#Adjective|major]] [[anniversary]] of an [[event#Noun|event]], [[particularly]] the [[fiftieth]] ([[50]][[-th|th]]) anniversary of a [[coronation]] or [[marriage]]. {{defdate|from late 14th c.}}
##* {{RQ:De Quincey Works|volume=I|chapter=The Female Infidel|section=footnote *|page=131|passage=[A] married couple, when celebrating the fiftieth anniversary of their marriage-day, are said to keep their ''golden'' '''jubilee''', but on the 25th anniversary they have credit only for a ''silver'' '''jubilee'''.}}
##* {{quote-journal|en|author=w:Mark Landler|title=Queen Elizabeth, anchor in a storm-tossed Britain, marks 70-year reign|newspaper=w:The New York Times|archiveurl=https://web.archive.org/web/20220206094650/https://www.nytimes.com/2022/02/06/world/europe/queen-elizabeth-platinum-jubilee.html|archivedate=6 February 2022|location=New York, N.Y.|publisher=w:The New York Times Company|date=6 February 2022|issn=0362-4331|oclc=971436363|passage=The 70th anniversary of Queen {{w|Elizabeth II}}’s accession to the throne will be a good deal more earthbound: The 95-year-old monarch plans to spend a quiet Sunday at her country estate, Sandringham, where her father died on Feb. 6, 1952. Four days of festivities to celebrate her Platinum '''Jubilee''' are scheduled for June.}}
# {{lb|en|figuratively}}
## {{lb|en|countable}} A [[time#Noun|time]] for [[release#Noun|release]] or [[restitution]]. {{defdate|from late 16th c.}}
##* {{RQ:Browne Religio Medici|edition=4th|section=44|page=94|passage=[T]hough it be in the povver of the vveakeſt arme to take avvay life, it is not in the ſtrongeſt to deprive us of death: {{...}} the firſt day of our '''Jubilee''' is death; the Devill hath therefore failed of his deſires; vvee are happier vvith death than vve ſhould have been vvithout it: there is no miſery but in himſelfe vvhere there is no end of miſery: {{...}}}}
##* {{quote-book|en|author=[[w:Henry Clay Work|[Henry Clay Work]]]|chapter=[[w:Marching Through Georgia|Marching through Georgia]]|title=Beadle’s Dime Song Book{{nb...|[...] A Collection of New and Popular Comic and Sentimental Songs.}}|location=New York, N.Y.|publisher=Beadle and Company,{{nb...|New York: 118 William Street. General dime book publishers.}}|month=(date written)|year=1865|year_published=1866|issue=17|page=57|pageurl=https://archive.org/details/beadles1018newy/page/n500/mode/1up|oclc=49962947|passage="Hurrah! hurrah! we bring the '''Jubilee'''! / Hurrah! Hurrah! the flag that makes you free!" / So we sung the chorus from Atlanta to the sea, / While we were marching through Georgia.}}
##* {{quote-book|en|author=L[evi] C. McKinstry|chapter=[Lincoln’s White Name]|title=A Poetic Offering to {{w|John Greenleaf Whittier}}{{nb...|[...] In Memoriam Dec. 17, 1807&nbsp;– 1834}}|location=Haverhill, Mass.|publisher=L. C. McKinstry|year=1890|page=101|pageurl=https://books.google.com/books?id=kbc-AAAAIAAJ&pg=PA101|oclc=17817633|passage=The chains of that great power we broke; / The burdened captives were set free, / For [[w:Abraham Lincoln|Lincoln]] held the pen, whose stroke / Proclaimed, the year of '''jubilee'''.}}
## {{lb|en|countable}} A time of [[celebration]] or [[rejoicing]]. {{defdate|from late 16th c.}}
##* {{RQ:Heywood Dramatic Works|volume=IV|play=A Maidenhead Well Lost|act=III|page=137|passage=But you firſt ſhall taſte / The bounty of our Court, with royall Preſents / Both to the Duke your maſter, and the Princeſſe; / It done, prepare we for this great ſolemnity, / Of Hymeneall '''Iubilies'''.}}
## {{lb|en|uncountable}} [[exultation|Exultation]], [[rejoicing#Noun|rejoicing]]; [[jubilation]]. {{defdate|from early 16th c.}}
##* {{RQ:Purchas Pilgrimes|part=3|chapter=A General Collection and Historicall Representation of the Iesuites Entrance into ''Iapon'' and ''China'', untill Their Admission in the Royal Citie of ''Nanquin''|section=II (''Iaponian'' Embassage to the Pope;{{nb...| of ''Nabunanga'' and ''Quabacondono'' Their Gouernment; ''Corai'' Inuaded, Embassage from ''China'', ''Taicosamas'' Temple, and ''Ogoshosamas'' Succession.}})|page=326|passage=Hee cauſed his little Sonne to goe vvith great State to ''Sandai'' to the ''Dairi'', that is, to bovv the head thrice before him dovvne to the Mats, vvho entertayned him vvith a ſolemne feaſt, vvith great '''Iubilee''' in alteration of names and titles of honour to the Nobles.}}
##* {{RQ:Shirley Coronation|page=29|passage=The peoples joy to knovv us reconcild, / Is added to the '''Iubile''' of the day, / VVe have no more a faction but one heart, / Peace flovv in every boſome.}}
##* {{RQ:Scott Peveril of the Peak|volume=I|chapter=III|pages=46–47|pageref=47|passage=Was it for Peveril of the Peak, in the '''jubilee''' of his spirits, to consider how his wife was to find beef and mutton to feast his neighbours?}}
##* {{RQ:Prescott Mexico|volume=III|chapter=VI|page=155|passage=The Mexicans, elated with their success, meanwhile, abandoned themselves to '''jubilee'''; singing, dancing, and feasting on the mangled relics of their wretched victims.}}
##* {{RQ:Tennyson Complete Poetical Works|poem=Sea Fairies|page=32|passage=We will kiss sweet kisses, and speak sweet words: / O listen, listen, your eyes shall glisten / With pleasure and love and '''jubilee''': {{...}}}}
## {{lb|en|uncountable}} The [[sound#Noun|sound]] of celebration or rejoicing; [[shout#Noun|shouts]] of [[joy#Noun|joy]]. {{defdate|from 16th c.}}
##* {{RQ:Milton Paradise Lost|book=III|page=80|lines=347–349|passage={{...}} Heav'n rung / VVith '''Jubilee''', and loud Hoſanna's fill'd / Th' eternal Regions: {{...}}|year=1873}}
##* {{RQ:Scott Lady of the Lake|canto=V|stanza=XXI|page=222|passage=[S]lowly down the steep descent / Fair Scotland's King and nobles went, / While all along the crowded way / Was '''jubilee''' and loud huzza.}}
## {{lb|en|countable|AAVE|music|often|attributively}} A [[joyful]] [[African-American]] (usually [[Christian]]) [[folk song]]. {{defdate|from late 19th c.}}
# {{lb|en|countable|obsolete}}
## A period of fifty years; a [[half-]][[century]]. {{defdate|17th–18th c.}}
##* {{RQ:Browne Pseudodoxia Epidemica|chapter=Of Credulity and Supinity|page=17|passage=Hovv their faiths could decline ſo low, as to concede their generations in heaven, to be made by the ſmell of a citron, or that the felicity of their Paradiſe ſhould conſiſt in a '''Jubile''' of conjunction, that is a coition of one act prolonged unto fifty years.}}
##* {{RQ:Fuller Church History|volume=I|section=II|sectionname=The Twelfth Century.{{nb...|Johanni Fitzjames de Leuston, in Comitatu Dorset. Armigero.}}|subsection=69 (Becket, after Fifty Years, Enshrined)|page=317|passage=And now, being on this subject, once to dispatch [[w:Thomas Becket|[Thomas] Becket]] out of our way, just a '''jubilee''' of years after his death, {{w|Stephen Langton}}, his mediate successor, removed his body from the Under-croft in Christ-church, where first he was buried, and laid him, at his own charge, in a most sumptuous shrine, at the east end of the church.}}
## {{lb|en|rare}} A fiftieth year. {{defdate|17th c.}}
##* {{RQ:Beaumont Fletcher Comedies and Tragedies|title=The Queen of Corinth|act=III|scene=i|page=11|column=1|passage=But is't poſſible he ſhould believe he is not of age? vvhy / He is 50, man, in's '''''Jubile''''' I vvarrant: s'light, he / Looks older then a groat, the very ſtamp on's face is / VVorne out vvith handling.}}

